.TH SDL_stack_alloc 3 "SDL 3.5.0" "Simple Directmedia Layer" "SDL3 FUNCTIONS"
.SH NAME
SDL_stack_alloc \- Allocate memory on the stack (maybe).
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <SDL3/SDL_stdinc.h>
.PP
.BI "#define SDL_stack_alloc(type, count)    (type*)alloca(sizeof(type)*(count))
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
If SDL knows how to access alloca() on the current platform, it will use it to stack-allocate memory here\[char46] If it doesn\(aqt, it will use
.BR SDL_malloc ()
to heap-allocate memory\[char46]
.PP
Since this might not be stack memory at all, it\(aqs important that you check the returned pointer for NULL, and that you call
.BR SDL_stack_free
on the memory when done with it\[char46] Since this might be stack memory, it\(aqs important that you don\(aqt allocate large amounts of it, or allocate in a loop without returning from the function, so the stack doesn\(aqt overflow\[char46]

.SH MACRO PARAMETERS
.TP
.I type
the datatype of the memory to allocate\[char46]
.TP
.I count
the number of
.BR type
objects to allocate\[char46]
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns newly-allocated memory, or NULL on failure\[char46]

.SH THREAD SAFETY
It is safe to call this macro from any thread\[char46]

.SH AVAILABILITY
This macro is available since SDL 3\[char46]2\[char46]0\[char46]

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR SDL_stack_free (3)
